  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual comparison of how photoexcited electrons and holes behave under different binding and transport conditions, and how that affects light emission or loss. Across the panels, incoming light creates an electron–hole pair. When binding is weak, the charges separate and move independently with winding paths that suggest diffusion. If defects or traps are present and mobility is high, the carriers can wander until they meet a defect, where non-radiative recombination occurs. With weak binding but no traps, the separated carriers remain mobile and can later recombine radiatively, producing emitted light.  \n\nA contrasting case shows strong binding, where the electron and hole stay together as an exciton rather than separating. This bound pair moves as a unit and can also emit light when it recombines.  \n\nThe last scenario returns to weak binding but adds many free carriers, representing traps plus low mobility or high injection. Here, a crowded population of separated charges appears, with both radiative recombination and defect-assisted recombination occurring. Overall, the figure highlights how the balance between carrier binding, mobility, trap density, and carrier concentration controls whether excitation ends in useful emission or loss at defects.",
